WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.213 --> 00:00:05.934
- Hello, welcome to the
Library of Congress.

00:00:05.934 --> 00:00:10.101
The Library of Congress is the
largest library in the world.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:15.543
- [Narrator] Located in Washington, D.C.,

00:00:15.543 --> 00:00:17.878
this collection of over 160 million items

00:00:17.878 --> 00:00:20.674
is a vast archive of the
history of the United States.

00:00:20.674 --> 00:00:22.089
There are over 70 special collections

00:00:22.089 --> 00:00:25.090
that include rare manuscripts,
maps, sheet music,

00:00:25.090 --> 00:00:26.257
and much more.

00:00:29.079 --> 00:00:33.188
- Hello, I'm Helena Zinkham,
Director for Collections

00:00:33.188 --> 00:00:36.873
and also Chief of the
Princeton Photographs Division.

00:00:36.873 --> 00:00:41.794
This collection of pictures
numbers more than 15 million.

00:00:41.794 --> 00:00:44.133
Among the rarest pictures
in our collection,

00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:45.842
we have the migrant mother,

00:00:45.842 --> 00:00:49.691
the iconic woman representing
the entire Great Depression.

00:00:49.691 --> 00:00:51.911
The pictures bring people together.

00:00:51.911 --> 00:00:55.302
They invite you in to look
around and explore worlds

00:00:55.302 --> 00:00:59.135
that you might never be
able to see in person.

00:01:00.723 --> 00:01:02.245
- Hello, I am Paulette Hasier.

00:01:02.245 --> 00:01:05.199
I am the chief of the
Geography and Map Division.

00:01:05.199 --> 00:01:09.366
We have 5.5 million maps,
80,000 atlases, 500 globes.

00:01:11.110 --> 00:01:14.206
We have Lewis and Clark's
maps, George Washington's maps.

00:01:14.206 --> 00:01:17.135
We have the first map that
shows the name America on it.

00:01:17.135 --> 00:01:19.684
We're part of the bigger, broader world.

00:01:19.684 --> 00:01:22.590
And as we look at some of
these cartographic evidence

00:01:22.590 --> 00:01:24.083
of the changes over time,

00:01:24.083 --> 00:01:26.987
it's a realization that we, as a nation,

00:01:26.987 --> 00:01:28.904
have changed over time.

00:01:30.796 --> 00:01:34.544
- Hi, I'm Sue Vita, the
chief of the Music Division.

00:01:34.544 --> 00:01:38.711
There are about 24 million
items in the music collection.

00:01:40.134 --> 00:01:42.507
We have Stradivari instruments.

00:01:42.507 --> 00:01:45.224
We have the original Rhapsody in Blue.

00:01:45.224 --> 00:01:48.520
The original edition of
the Star Spangled Banner.

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:52.052
Music tells us more about who we are

00:01:52.052 --> 00:01:55.635
and tells us about our
past and our future.

00:01:58.330 --> 00:02:00.109
- Hi, my name is Michael North,

00:02:00.109 --> 00:02:02.157
and I'm head of Reference
and Reader Services

00:02:02.157 --> 00:02:04.311
in the Rare Book and
Special Collections Division

00:02:04.311 --> 00:02:06.195
at the Library of Congress.

00:02:06.195 --> 00:02:09.130
This collection holds over 800,000 items.

00:02:09.130 --> 00:02:11.967
We have everything from
medieval manuscripts,

00:02:11.967 --> 00:02:15.822
early printed books, the
libraries of Thomas Jefferson.

00:02:15.822 --> 00:02:19.264
One of our most famous books
is the Gutenberg Bible.

00:02:19.264 --> 00:02:21.283
It's important to have
a record of the past

00:02:21.283 --> 00:02:23.800
so that we know how we came
to be where we are today,

00:02:23.800 --> 00:02:25.456
both in terms of ideas

00:02:25.456 --> 00:02:29.623
and in terms of history,
politics, art, everything.

00:02:31.032 --> 00:02:32.555
- Hi, I'm Malea Walker.

00:02:32.555 --> 00:02:33.574
I'm a reference librarian

00:02:33.574 --> 00:02:37.289
in the Serial and Government
Publications Division.

00:02:37.289 --> 00:02:39.676
This collection is massive.

00:02:39.676 --> 00:02:42.918
We have 800,000 reels of microfilm.

00:02:42.918 --> 00:02:47.085
We have about 7.2 million
loose issues of newspapers

00:02:48.781 --> 00:02:52.948
and approximately 42,000 volumes
of bound newspaper pages.

00:02:54.112 --> 00:02:58.195
We also have about 140,000
issues of comic books.

00:02:59.175 --> 00:03:01.469
One of the great things
about reading newspapers

00:03:01.469 --> 00:03:04.592
is that you get a sense of what
it's like to be an American.

00:03:04.592 --> 00:03:08.759
You are reading how people
lived the history itself.

00:03:10.239 --> 00:03:14.406
- Collections are our way of
adding memory and remembrances.

00:03:16.796 --> 00:03:21.436
All of the collections
give meaning and context

00:03:21.436 --> 00:03:25.603
to the human experience
and the history of culture.

